      <TableCell>Yes</TableCell>
      <TableCell>Scan timestamp</TableCell>
      <TableCell>String</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>description</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>No</TableCell>
      <TableCell>Short description</TableCell>
      <TableCell>String</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>reference</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Yes</TableCell>
      <TableCell>URL of information source or more details. Required so user might validate the data.</TableCell>
      <TableCell>String</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>scannerVersion</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>No</TableCell>
      <TableCell>Scanner version</TableCell>
      <TableCell>String</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>compliant</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>No</TableCell>
      <TableCell>Compliance pass or fail</TableCell>
      <TableCell>Boolean</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>summary</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>No</TableCell>
      <TableCell>Summary of issues found</TableCell>
      <TableCell>List (see table below)</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      </TableBody>
      </Table>
      <p>The <code>summary</code> field must adhere to the following format:</p>
      <p><strong>Summary field value format</strong></p>
      <Table class="my-4">
      <TableHeader>
      <TableRow>
      <TableHead>Field</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Description</TableHead>
      <TableHead>Type</TableHead>
      </TableRow>
      </TableHeader>
      <TableBody>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>label</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Display label for component (for example, "critical," "important," "moderate," "low," or "health")</TableCell>
      <TableCell>String</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>data</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Data for this component (for example, count of vulnerabilities found or score)</TableCell>
      <TableCell>String</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>severityIndex</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>Component index allowing for ordering and assigning graphical representation. The value is range <code>0..3</code> where <code>0</code> = low.</TableCell>
      <TableCell>Integer</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      <TableRow>
      <TableCell><code>reference</code></TableCell>
      <TableCell>URL of information source or more details. Optional.</TableCell>
      <TableCell>String</TableCell>
      </TableRow>
      </TableBody>
      </Table>
      <h4 id="security-example-annotation-values_security-container-content" class="group scroll-mt-20">Example annotation values<a href="#security-example-annotation-values_security-container-content" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h4>
      <p>This example shows an OpenSCAP annotation for an image with vulnerability summary data and a compliance boolean:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"{\n  \"name\": \"OpenSCAP\",\n  \"description\": \"OpenSCAP vulnerability score\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2016-09-08T05:04:46Z\",\n  \"reference\": \"https://www.open-scap.org/930492\",\n  \"compliant\": true,\n  \"scannerVersion\": \"1.2\",\n  \"summary\": [\n    { \"label\": \"critical\", \"data\": \"4\", \"severityIndex\": 3, \"reference\": null },\n    { \"label\": \"important\", \"data\": \"12\", \"severityIndex\": 2, \"reference\": null },\n    { \"label\": \"moderate\", \"data\": \"8\", \"severityIndex\": 1, \"reference\": null },\n    { \"label\": \"low\", \"data\": \"26\", \"severityIndex\": 0, \"reference\": null }\n  ]\n}"} lang="json" title="OpenSCAP annotation" />
      <p>This example shows the <a href="https://catalog.redhat.com/software/containers/explore">Container images section of the Red Hat Ecosystem Catalog</a> annotation for an image with health index data with an external URL for additional details:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"{\n  \"name\": \"Red Hat Ecosystem Catalog\",\n  \"description\": \"Container health index\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"2016-09-08T05:04:46Z\",\n  \"reference\": \"https://access.redhat.com/errata/RHBA-2016:1566\",\n  \"compliant\": null,\n  \"scannerVersion\": \"1.2\",\n  \"summary\": [\n    { \"label\": \"Health index\", \"data\": \"B\", \"severityIndex\": 1, \"reference\": null }\n  ]\n}"} lang="json" title="Red Hat Ecosystem Catalog annotation" />
      <h3 id="security-annotating-image-objects_security-container-content" class="group scroll-mt-20">Annotating image objects<a href="#security-annotating-image-objects_security-container-content" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>While image stream objects are what a user of OpenShift Container Platform operates against, image objects are annotated with security metadata. Image objects are cluster-scoped, pointing to a single image that might be referenced by many image streams and tags.</p>
      <h4 id="security-example-annotate-CLI_security-container-content" class="group scroll-mt-20">Example annotate CLI command<a href="#security-example-annotate-CLI_security-container-content" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h4>
      <p>Replace <code>&lt;image&gt;</code> with an image digest, for example <code>sha256:401e359e0f45bfdcf004e258b72e253fd07fba8cc5c6f2ed4f4608fb119ecc2</code>:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc annotate image <image> \\\n    quality.images.openshift.io/vulnerability.redhatcatalog='{ \\\n    \"name\": \"Red Hat Ecosystem Catalog\", \\\n    \"description\": \"Container health index\", \\\n    \"timestamp\": \"2020-06-01T05:04:46Z\", \\\n    \"compliant\": null, \\\n    \"scannerVersion\": \"1.2\", \\\n    \"reference\": \"https://access.redhat.com/errata/RHBA-2020:2347\", \\\n    \"summary\": \"[ \\\n      { \"label\": \"Health index\", \"data\": \"B\", \"severityIndex\": 1, \"reference\": null } ]\" }'"} lang="terminal" />
      <h3 id="controlling-pod-execution_security-container-content" class="group scroll-mt-20">Controlling pod execution<a href="#controlling-pod-execution_security-container-content" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>Use the <code>images.openshift.io/deny-execution</code> image policy to programmatically control if an image can be run.</p>
      <h4 id="security-controlling-pod-execution-example-annotation_security-container-content" class="group scroll-mt-20">Example annotation<a href="#security-controlling-pod-execution-example-annotation_security-container-content" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h4>
      <CodeBlock code={"annotations:\n  images.openshift.io/deny-execution: true"} lang="yaml" />
      <h3 id="security-integration-reference_security-container-content" class="group scroll-mt-20">Integration reference<a href="#security-integration-reference_security-container-content" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h3>
      <p>In most cases, external tools such as vulnerability scanners develop a script or plugin that watches for image updates, performs scanning, and annotates the associated image object with the results. Typically this automation calls the OpenShift Container Platform 4.22 REST APIs to write the annotation. See OpenShift Container Platform REST APIs for general information about the REST APIs.</p>
      <h4 id="security-integration-reference-example-api-call_security-container-content" class="group scroll-mt-20">Example REST API call<a href="#security-integration-reference-example-api-call_security-container-content" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h4>
      <p>The following example call by using <code>curl</code> overrides the value of the annotation. Be sure to replace the values for <code>&lt;token&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;openshift_server&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;image_id&gt;</code>, and <code>&lt;image_annotation&gt;</code>.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"$ curl -X PATCH \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer <token>\" \\\n  -H \"Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json\" \\\n  https://<openshift_server>:6443/apis/image.openshift.io/v1/images/<image_id> \\\n  --data '{ <image_annotation> }'"} lang="terminal" title="Patch API call" />
      <p>The following is an example of <code>PATCH</code> payload data:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"{\n\"metadata\": {\n  \"annotations\": {\n    \"quality.images.openshift.io/vulnerability.redhatcatalog\":\n       \"{ 'name': 'Red Hat Ecosystem Catalog', 'description': 'Container health index', 'timestamp': '2020-06-01T05:04:46Z', 'compliant': null, 'reference': 'https://access.redhat.com/errata/RHBA-2020:2347', 'summary': [{'label': 'Health index', 'data': '4', 'severityIndex': 1, 'reference': null}] }\"\n    }\n  }\n}"} lang="terminal" title="Patch call data" />
